  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A+x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A+0} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2+x}.\n$$",
